## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft a compact DP with a weighted subsequence objective, a common class of DP optimization.

## Key Concepts
- 1D/2D DP for subsequences; reconstruction; rolling optimization.
- Transition with weight t.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxWeightedSubsequence(A, k){
  const n = A.length; // 1-indexed in problem
  const dp = Array.from({length:k+1}, ()=> Array(n+1).fill(-Infinity));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[0][i]=0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      dp[t][i] = Math.max(dp[t][i-1], dp[t-1][i-1] + t*A[i-1]);
    }
  }
  // backtrack omitted for brevity
  return {value: dp[k][n]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the indices efficiently? 
- How does space optimize with a rolling array?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with an extra resource dimension (shields) on a classic grid path. Demonstrates handling of constrained resources, 3D DP states, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an additional dimension for resources (k)
- Transition with/without consuming a shield
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<m; j++) {
      for (let k=0; k<=K; k++) {
        const v = grid[i][j];
        if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k] + v);
        if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k] + v);
        if (k>0 && v < 0) {
          if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k-1]);
          if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover one optimal path and its moves?
- How to optimize space to O(n*m*K)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes a standard DP-on-partitions with a nonlinear segment cost. It tests formulating a clean O(n^2 k) DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP over prefixes
- Prefix sums for segment sums
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity considerations and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartitionSquares(arr, k){
  const n = arr.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + arr[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const opt = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p])*(pref[i]-pref[p]);
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; opt[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const segs = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const p = opt[i][t];
    segs.unshift([p+1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], segments: segs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt with a per-segment penalty or bonus c?
- Can we accelerate with divide-and-conquer optimization if quadrangle inequality holds?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes DP with non-overlapping blocks and a non-additive subarray score (bitwise OR). It requires a state that handles gaps and a compact representation of OR values, plus a reconstruction strategy.

## Key Concepts
- DP over k blocks with a required gap
- Distinct ORs per end i optimization (≤ O(log MAXA))
- Backtracking pointers for block endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs and practical constants

## Code Example
```javascript
// DP sketch
for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
  const ors = orStates[i]; // [{val,left}, ...]
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const {val,left} of ors) {
      const prev = (left > 1) ? dp[left-2][t-1] : 0;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], prev + val);
      parent[i][t] = { left, val };
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a variable gap g between blocks and for weighted gaps?
- Can you extend the approach to allow a bonus if a block ends at a specific index?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft DP with interval constraints and reconstruction using palindromic substrings.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic substring precomputation in O(n^2)
- DP over prefix length and exact count: dp[i][t]
- Transition considers ending a palindrome at i with start p
- Reconstruction requires backpointers to recover substrings

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalSubstr(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; ++j) {
      if (s[i] === s[j] && (j - i < 2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j] = true;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let p = 0; p < i; p++) {
          if (isPal[p][i-1]) {
            const val = dp[p][t-1] + (i - p);
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction would trace par[] to extract substrings
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) space?
- How would you reconstruct the exact substrings efficiently without extra passes?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint—a fundamental DP pattern in scheduling and resource allocation. It also tests the ability to reconstruct the actual solution, not just compute the optimal value.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with an exact cardinality constraint
- Precomputing p(i) via binary search after sorting intervals by their right endpoint
- Two-dimensional DP table indexed by interval position and number selected
- Backtracking through the DP table to recover the chosen intervals

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKIntervals(intervals, A, k) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a.r - b.r);
    const n = intervals.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const selected = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        const [l, r] = [intervals[i-1].l, intervals[i-1].r];
        const v = intervals[i-1].value;
        const p = findPrev(intervals, i-1);

        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            if (dp[p][t-1] + v > dp[i-1][t]) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + v;
                selected[i][t] = true;
            } else {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
        if (selected[i][t]) {
            result.unshift(i - 1);
            t--;
            i = p;
        } else {
            i--;
        }
    }

    return { maxValue: dp[n][k], indices: result };
}

function findPrev(intervals, idx) {
    let lo = 0, hi = idx;
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (intervals[mid].r <= intervals[idx].l) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Preprocessing p(i): O(n log n) via sorting and binary search
- DP computation: O(n × k)
- Reconstruction: O(n + k)
- Space: O(n × k) for the DP table

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new DP variant: maximizing the sum of non-additive subarray values (bitwise OR) while enforcing exactly k non-overlapping blocks. It also probes reconstruction logic for end-to-start blocks.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitioning into k non-overlapping blocks
- Non-additive block value via OR, requiring on-the-fly accumulation
- Backpointers for exact block endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKOrSubarrays(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      let curr=0;
      for(let j=i;j>=0;j--){
        curr |= A[j];
        let left = dp[j][t-1];
        if(left>-Infinity){
          const cand = left + curr;
          if(cand>dp[i+1][t]){ dp[i+1][t]=cand; prv[i+1][t]=j; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {value: dp[n][k], endAt: prv};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct all k blocks from the backpointers?
- How might you optimize to O(nk) space or prune inner loop with bit tricks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests time-expanded DP on grids for path counts with constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) layers; space optimization; modulo arithmetic.
- Handling walls and boundary checks.

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[n-1][m-1] === 1) return 0;
  let prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
  prev[0][0] = 1;
  const dirs = [[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const cur = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (const [dx,dy] of dirs) {
        const x = i + dx, y = j + dy;
        if (x>=0 && x<n && y>=0 && y<m && grid[x][y]===0) {
          cur[i][j] = (cur[i][j] + prev[x][y]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    prev = cur;
  }
  return prev[n-1][m-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a non-trivial partition DP with a substring cost that requires precomputation and reconstruction, combining both combinatorial choices and an aggregate cost function.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions with a cost function over substrings
- Efficient precomputation of substring distinct counts
- Backtracking reconstruction of cut points
- Time/space trade-offs and memory optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: precompute cost[p][i], then DP with backpointers
const n = s.length;
const INF = 1e9;
const cost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
for (let p = 0; p < n; p++) {
  const seen = new Array(26).fill(false);
  let distinct = 0;
  for (let i = p; i < n; i++) {
    const idx = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
    if (!seen[idx]) { seen[idx] = true; distinct++; }
    cost[p][i] = distinct;
  }
}
const kCap = Math.min(k, n);
const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(kCap+1).fill(INF));
const prv = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(kCap+1).fill(-1));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, kCap); t++) {
    for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
      const cand = dp[p][t-1] + cost[p][i-1];
      if (cand < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; prv[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This introduces selecting non-covering, fixed-cost segments, a natural DP with a running max optimization. It tests understanding of prefix sums, state pruning, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums to compute segment sums in O(1)
- DP over i and t with exactly t segments
- Running maximum best[t] to avoid O(n) inner loop
- Path reconstruction to recover endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueSegments(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i]=S[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(negInf));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill([-1,-1]));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) par[0][t]=[-1,-1];
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best = negInf; // max of dp[m][t-1]-S[m]
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      best = Math.max(best, dp[i-1][t-1]-S[i-1]);
      const val = best + S[i] - c;
      if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=[i-1,-1]; }
      if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t]=[i-1,t]; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction (simple version)
  let i=n, t=k, ends=[];
  while(t>0){
    const [pi, pt] = par[i][t] || [-1,-1];
    if(pi===-1) break;
    // approximate end at i, start at some m where dp[m][t-1]-S[m] was max
    // for brevity here, return only values; full reconstruction can be added as needed
    ends.push(i);
    i = pi; t--;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], ends: ends.reverse() };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k segments or to return actual segment endpoints precisely?
- What is the time/space complexity and how can you optimize further for large n?

